<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quanzhen School</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1160s–</span> · Also known as Complete Perfection, Quanzhen Daoism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Quanzhen (Complete Perfection) School is a branch of Daoism founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-chongyang">Wang Chongyang</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the Jin dynasty in the 1160s. Drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, it stressed celibacy, monastic life, meditation and inner alchemy. Its leader Qiu Chuji met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> in 1222, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>' favour it spread widely. With headquarters at the White Cloud Temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, it is one of the two main branches of Daoism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quanzhen-school.jpg" alt="Quanzhen School" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo taken by User:Vmenkov. Painter's name is not known to the photographer. Although there is a fair amount of sign... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Changchun-Temple-Master-and-disciples-painting-0316.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
